WEBVTT

NOTE Captions timed to the LibriVox recording of "I Hear America Singing" (Walt Whitman), read by Zach Meissner.

1
00:00.500 --> 00:10.030
[Spoken introduction]

2
00:11.240 --> 00:12.840
I hear America singing,

3
00:13.070 --> 00:15.070
the varied carols I hear,

4
00:15.610 --> 00:17.110
Those of mechanics,

5
00:17.220 --> 00:20.100
each one singing his as it should be blithe and strong,

6
00:20.750 --> 00:24.220
The carpenter singing his as he measures his plank or beam,

7
00:24.820 --> 00:27.930
The mason singing his as he makes ready for work,

8
00:28.180 --> 00:29.410
or leaves off work,

9
00:30.230 --> 00:33.380
The boatman singing what belongs to him in his boat,

10
00:33.800 --> 00:36.260
the deckhand singing on the steamboat deck,

11
00:36.970 --> 00:39.740
The shoemaker singing as he sits on his bench,

12
00:40.110 --> 00:42.130
the hatter singing as he stands,

13
00:43.040 --> 00:44.390
The wood-cutter's song,

14
00:44.710 --> 00:46.960
the ploughboy's on his way in the morning,

15
00:47.260 --> 00:48.650
or at noon intermission

16
00:48.830 --> 00:49.840
or at sundown,

17
00:50.560 --> 00:52.480
The delicious singing of the mother,

18
00:52.950 --> 00:54.550
or of the young wife at work,

19
00:54.950 --> 00:56.970
or of the girl sewing or washing,

20
00:57.930 --> 01:01.770
Each singing what belongs to him or her and to none else,

21
01:02.270 --> 01:04.380
The day what belongs to the day—

22
01:04.680 --> 01:08.450
at night the party of young fellows, robust, friendly,

23
01:08.940 --> 01:12.660
Singing with open mouths their strong melodious songs.

24
01:14.080 --> 01:17.490
[Spoken closing]

